/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The full-wave (bridge) doubler puts two equal capacitors C1 and C2 in series across the load, with their midpoint joined to one supply terminal.
   - Vm is the peak supply voltage in V, Idc the load current in A, f the supply frequency in Hz and C the value of each capacitor in F.
   - Why Idc/(fC)?
   - The half-wave doubler (a clamp followed by a peak rectifier) puts C1 in series with the supply.
   - Six diodes, two per phase: D1, D3, D5 have their cathodes joined to DC+, and D4, D6, D2 their anodes joined to DC−.
   - VL is the line-to-line RMS voltage and VLm = √2 VL its peak, in V.
   - The output swings between VLm (the top of each cap) and VLm cos 30° = 0.866 VLm, six times per cycle.
   - With a large DC inductor the load current is a flat Id.
   - The three-phase half-wave (three-pulse) diode circuit has one diode per phase and returns the load to the neutral: Vdc = (3√3/2π) Vm = 1.17 Vph (Vm the phase peak, Vph the phase RMS), with ripple at 3f and DC in the transformer windings.
*/
