/* book: lead: Replace the diodes of a rectifier with thyristors and every turn-on can be delayed by a firing angle α. The average output then falls as α grows, and with a load that keeps current flowing it can even turn negative. Battery chargers, DC motor drives and electrolysis supplies work this way. This chapter covers the one-pulse (half-wave), half-controlled and fully controlled two-pulse circuits with R */
/* book Figure 2.4.1: The worked example’s bridge at α = 60° on 230 V. With R alone (a) the output is zero until each firing, averaging 155.3 V; with a large inductance (b) T1 and T2 carry the current on until T3 and T4 fire at 240°, adding a negative slice and pulling the average down to 103.5 V. */
