/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A thyristor waits for a gate pulse.
   - Half-wave (one-pulse) circuit, R load.
   - Vdc is the average and Vrms the RMS load voltage in V; Vm is the peak supply voltage in V; α is the firing angle.
   - RL load.
   - RLE load (battery charging).
   - Four thyristors in a bridge: T1 and T2 form one diagonal and are fired at α; T3 and T4 form the other and are fired at π + α.
   - On a 230 V supply 2Vm/π = 207.07 V, the most the bridge can give.
   - With an R load the current stops at π, so the output is zero from 0 to α in each half-cycle.
   - The current is continuous when the load inductance can carry it from one firing to the next.
   - Above α = 90° the formula gives a negative Vdc while the current keeps its direction, so power flows from the DC side to the AC supply.
*/
